.LP
According to Plato, Anaxagoras was one of the first to propose that whatever there is,
is because that this is the best way.
In
.I Phaedo
Plato lets Socrates tell a story
.QP
.ps 10
.vs 12
One day I heard someone reading,
as he said,
from a book of Anaxagoras,
and saying that it is the Mind that directs and is the cause of everything.
I was delighted with this cause and it seemed to me good,
in a way,
that Mind should be the cause of all.
I thought that if this were so,
the directing Mind would direct everything and arrange each thing in the way that was best.
.[ (
Plato Phaedo Cooper
.], Phaedo 97b-99d)
.LP
This might be one of the earliest formulation of the idea that things are arranged the way they are because it is the result of a process leading to a result which is predictable because it is the \f[I]best\f[P] result.
However, this begs two questions:
(i) Who or what is the cause,
the Mind?
(ii) In what respect is the result the best one?
.PP
Anaxagoras describes the Mind, the
.I Nous,
as the principle that bring order to the cosmic chaos.
The teleological explanation seems natural to us humans,
we search for purpose.
.[
 Deborah Kelemen 2000
.]
.[
Kodaj defense of teleological intuitions 2023
.]
.PP
Ruth Garrett Millikan is the pioneer in building a modern theory of functions,
which is built on a solid ground.
She thinks of functions as things that are somehow designed for a purpose.
.QP
.vs 12
.ps 10
My claim will be that it is the \[lq]proper function\[rq] of a thing that puts it in a biological category,
and this has to do not with its powers but with its history.
Having a proper function is a matter of having been \[lq]designed to\[rq] or of being \[lq]supposed to\[rq] (impersonal) perform a certain function.
The task of the theory of proper functions is to define this sense of \[lq]designed to\[rq] or \[lq]supposed to\[rq] in naturalist, nonnormative, and nonmysterious terms.
.[ (
Millikan language thought 
.], p. 17)
.so proper-functions.ms
.PP
A biological category is given by its proper function.
